[flake8]
# WPS412 forbids logic in an __init__.py and WPS410 forbids module-level
# metadata such as __all__. Eight package initializers are scoped exemptions
# here: the root one for the package metadata it declares, and seven that
# deliberately carry import-time logic.
#
# * orchestrator/__init__.py is the whole of the root package: it declares the
#   distribution version and the explicit __all__ (WPS410) naming it, and binds
#   nothing else, so `import orchestrator` costs this module and no owner
#   behind it. Both assignments read as logic to WPS412, which is waived for
#   that reason.
# * orchestrator/config/__init__.py invokes the `environment` resolver and
#   binds its results so reloading or patching `orchestrator.config` re-runs
#   resolution into the same namespace the helpers read -- the reload / patch
#   contract this package exists to preserve -- and publishes its narrow
#   public surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) in that initializer.
# * orchestrator/agents/__init__.py is the API an agent run is driven through:
#   it re-exports the model types, the runner owner's `run_agent`, and the
#   process owner's `terminate_all_running`, and publishes that narrow public
#   surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so a caller spawns an agent
#   through the package. WPS412 is waived for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/github/__init__.py is the PyGithub client API: it re-exports
#   the composed `GitHubClient` and the pinned durable-state model from their
#   owner modules, and publishes that narrow public surface through an explicit
#   __all__ (WPS410) so a caller reaches the client through the package. WPS412
#   is waived for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/scheduler/__init__.py is the scheduler API: it re-exports the
#   concrete `IssueScheduler` from the `service` owner and the caller-facing
#   `SubmissionRequest` from the `models` owner, and publishes that narrow
#   public surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so a caller submits
#   through the package. WPS412 is waived for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/workflow/__init__.py is the workflow package API: it
#   re-exports five names from the `state` owner -- the two label vocabularies,
#   the transition guard and the predicate under it, and the illegal-write
#   exception -- and defines the per-repo `tick` entry point that resolves the
#   engine inside the call so the GitHub and git layers importing `state`
#   beside it never pay for the engine graph. It publishes that narrow public
#   surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410), and WPS412 is waived for the
#   import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/observability/usage/__init__.py is the usage-parsing API: it
#   re-exports the nine parsers defined across the `metrics`, `skills`, and
#   `trajectory` owners, a per-backend trio each, plus the six result types
#   they hand back -- two from those same owners and the
#   trajectory quartet from `trajectory_models` -- and publishes that narrow
#   public surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so a caller reaches
#   the parsers through the package. WPS412 is waived for that import-time
#   logic.
# * orchestrator/observability/analytics/recording/__init__.py is the analytics
#   recording API: it re-exports the six recorders a producer
#   calls -- the record envelope and the sink append beneath it, plus one each
#   for a stage entered, a stage evaluated, a repo's skill catalog scanned,
#   and a tracked agent run finished, five from the `events` owner and the
#   sequenced one from `agent_exit` -- and publishes that narrow public
#   surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so a producer appends through
#   the package rather than reaching past it. WPS412 is waived for that
#   import-time logic. Every other initializer under `observability/` stays a
#   marker that binds nothing.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/state.py is one owner for the typed workflow state --
# the label and control vocabularies, the transition graph keyed by them, and
# the coercion and guard every label write passes through -- so it carries 11
# members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7. Splitting the graph away from the labels
# it is keyed by, or the guard away from the graph it reads, would put the state
# machine back across the flat leaves this owner replaces, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/comments.py is one owner for both directions of
# the issue/PR comment surface -- the marker and id bookkeeping every posted
# comment goes through, and the trust-filtered read the agent prompts quote --
# so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling. The two directions are one
# policy: the marker the writer stamps is what the reader filters on, and
# splitting them would let the pair drift apart, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/messages.py is one owner for the whole agent
# message contract -- the markers read out of a last message and the stderr
# diagnostics written back when there is nothing usable in it -- so it carries
# 10 members past the same ceiling. Each marker is a small strict parser, and
# the prompt that promises one is the prompt that also promises the park the
# others fall to, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/prompts.py is one owner for the prompt builders
# the workflow stages share, so it carries 15 members past the same ceiling.
# They share one opening header and one set of notes, and each one's marker
# contract is what another owner then parses; splitting them by stage would let
# the prompt and the parser that reads its answer drift apart, so WPS202 is
# waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/usage.py is one owner for everything a tracked
# agent run is accounted by -- the audit pair and analytics record the spawn is
# bookended with, and the per-issue counters the usage that record parses is
# then folded into -- so it carries 10 members past the same ceiling and names
# 14 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. The fold consumes the `UsageMetrics`
# the record produced, so splitting the halves apart would put one run's
# accounting across two modules, and the import list is just the union of what
# each half is typed by: the analytics sink, the runner owner the spawn
# dispatches on, and the agent result on one side, the issue, the pinned state,
# and the comment poster on the other. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/dispatch.py is one owner for everything between
# a repo's pollable issues and a running stage handler -- the hard-skip filter,
# the family / fanout partition and its cap-exemption probe, the per-worker
# refetch, the scheduler submits, and the timed per-issue dispatch -- so it
# carries 15 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 16 imports past the
# WPS201 ceiling of 12. Each link is only safe given the one before it: the
# filter is what keeps a parked issue from flipping the bucket cap-counted, and
# the partition is what the exemption probe reads, so splitting them would put
# one tick's concurrency contract across several modules. The import list is
# the union of what that chain is typed by -- the issue and client it routes,
# the labels it filters on, the scheduler it submits to, and the analytics sink
# it times into. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/tick.py is one owner for a single repo's polling
# pass -- the base refresh, the community-contribution sweep, the skill-catalog
# emission, the scheduler handoff, and the two in-tick execution modes -- so it
# carries 11 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 14 imports past the
# WPS201 ceiling of 12. The order those passes run in IS the behavior a tick is
# defined by, so a pass hoisted into its own module would leave that order
# unreadable from any one of them; the sweep in particular has no per-issue home
# because the PRs it labels carry no pinned state. The import list is the union
# of what one pass needs -- the executor and semaphore the fanout is bounded by,
# the client and spec every pass is typed by, the base-sync owner the opening
# refresh runs on, the label the sweep writes, the catalog owner the skill
# emission calls, and the scheduler the handoff submits to. WPS201 and WPS202
# are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/drift.py is one owner for what a user-content
# change is and what it costs -- the hash, the six filters that keep it from
# moving on content no human wrote, and the two routes a real move is handed
# to -- so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling. The routes are the only
# reason the hash exists, and a filter loosened without the route that fires on
# it is a re-decompose nobody asked for, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/terminals.py is one owner for every way an issue
# stops being worked -- the merged, rejected, and human-closed arcs, the shared
# context and cleanup they run through, and the two entry points the stages
# reach them by -- so it carries 13 members past the same ceiling and names 13
# imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. The arcs share one tail (terminal
# stamp, terminal label, usage receipt, single pinned write) and differ only in
# what the PR's own state permits afterwards, so splitting them would let two
# copies of that order drift apart. The import list is what one arc is typed by:
# the issue, client, and pinned state it reads and writes, the label it lands
# on, the usage receipt it posts, the branch name and worktree teardown a dead
# branch goes through, and the GitHub issue-state vocabulary the closed-issue
# arc both reads and writes. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# The decomposition stage owners under orchestrator/workflow/stages/ pay two
# ceilings between them. `split.py` carries 11 members past the WPS202 ceiling
# of 7 and `validation.py` carries 11 too: the order the split writes children
# in IS its crash-safety contract -- a create hoisted away from the parent-state
# flush that follows it reads as safe from either module -- and every rule a
# `split` payload must satisfy is checked before the first irreversible
# `create_child_issue`, so the bound, the child shapes, and the graph they form
# are one gate rather than three. Six of the owners also name more than the
# WPS201 ceiling of 12 imports -- `blocked.py`, `outcomes.py`, `run.py`,
# `session.py`, `split.py`, and `umbrella.py` -- because a stage owner is typed
# by the whole span it acts on: the issue and client it reads, the pinned state
# and labels it writes, the engine's comment, guard, prompt, and usage surfaces
# it publishes through, the git owners the read-only decomposer checkout is
# opened, probed, and torn down through, and the sibling and implementing owners
# it hands a `single` verdict off to. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived for those
# files.
#
# Six validating stage owners name more than the same WPS201 ceiling of 12 for
# the same reason -- `approval.py`, `awaiting.py`, `dev_fix.py`, `drift.py`,
# `requested_changes.py`, and `reviewer.py`. Each of them spans one agent run
# end to end, so it is typed by the issue, client, and pinned state it reads and
# writes, the engine surfaces it comments, guards, prompts, and meters through,
# the sibling owners it disposes the run to, the implementing and publication
# owners it borrows the dev resume, the session read, the parks, and the squash
# from, and one entry per git owner it calls: the branch and worktree names, the
# checkout creation, the HEAD and dirty probes, the authenticated fetch and
# push, and the ahead/behind measurement are each a separate owner, so a route
# that resolves a branch, reads HEAD, and pushes names three. Splitting any of
# them by import would put one run's decisions across two modules. WPS201 is
# waived for those files.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/in_review/drift.py names 16 for the same reason
# and one more: the body-edit route owns neither half of the run it drives, so
# on top of the engine's comment, drift, guard, and usage surfaces it borrows
# the dev resume from the implementing owners and the reply disposition from
# the validating ones, and names the three git owners behind the checkout it
# resolves, recreates, and watermarks. WPS201 is waived for that file.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/discussion/handler.py is one tick asked in the
# order its questions have to be asked, and it carries 1 member past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7: the round and its disposition, the resume a reply drives, the
# hold that refuses to open one on a checkout nobody may open on, the single
# reading both of those are decided from, and the three settles that reading
# fans out to -- a commit under this stage's own park, a checkout that has
# moved off the anchor, and the recovered commit both of them share -- behind
# the entry point that orders the lot. Splitting the settles from the order
# they are asked in would put the ownership rule (a commit is this stage's only
# while one of its rounds is in flight) in a second module from the reading it
# is applied to, and those two drifting apart is exactly how a foreign stage's
# commit gets published as a plan. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/discussion/parks.py is one owner for every way
# that stage hands an issue back, and carries 22 members past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7: the funnel (`_park_discussion`), the seven text builders its
# messages share (`_paths_markdown` for the bounded path list the dirty,
# stranded, and refused-commit parks quote, `_reset_target` for the SHA the
# commit parks tell an operator to reset to, `_committed_reading` /
# `_artifact_reading` / `_unpublishable_plan_message` for the reading of the
# branch the two refusals
# of a committed artifact share, and `_stale_publication_standing` /
# `_stale_publication_remedy` for the two halves a stale publication's message
# differs by -- whether the remote still carries the commit decides both what
# is true of it and whether dropping it is a local reset at all), and one
# builder each for the fourteen ways a
# round ends or fails to open -- dirty, stranded, unreadable, timed out,
# refused commit,
# recovered refused commit, a commit no round of this stage was in flight to
# have made, blocked-resume, silent, and the response itself,
# plus the four the plan publication reaches: the PR it opened, the push that
# failed, the publication whose commit the branch has moved off, and the plan
# no session was recorded for. Two of those builders carry a second message
# rather than a second member: a checkout `git status` could not report on
# blocks a round the same way a dirty one does, from the same two sites, and
# has to say the opposite thing -- no paths to quote, no tip to reset to -- so
# the wording that differs stays beside the wording it differs from. The
# blocked-resume one is the odd member: no round ran, but a
# human's reply into a checkout no round may open on needs the same paths and
# the same reset command every other park quotes, and assembling it anywhere
# else would put one hand-back's wording outside the funnel that stamps its
# reason. The reason each park records is not just
# its message: the handler reads the `discussion_` prefix back on the next
# tick to decide whose turn it is, so a park assembled anywhere other than
# behind the funnel that stamps it would earn a second round over the top of
# the first, and a builder split off from the parks that quote it would put
# one hand-back's wording across two modules. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/discussion/publication.py names 13 imports past
# the WPS201 ceiling of 12 for the reason every publishing owner does: it spans
# one artifact from the branch it is read off to the PR it lands on, so it is
# typed by the stage's own carriers, keys, parks, round-checkout restorer, and
# session read, and by one git owner per step -- the branch and worktree names,
# the HEAD, status, committed-path, and tree probes, the authenticated push,
# the first-commit subject, and the PR title builders are each their own owner,
# so a module that resolves a branch, reads four probes off it, pushes it, and
# titles its PR pays six imports for one paragraph of code. Reaching them
# through a re-export site instead would put a module that defines nothing
# between the caller and the helper it runs, so WPS201 is waived.
#
# It carries 25 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 for the same span: the
# checkout it reads (`_plan_worktree`), the reading itself -- where the branch
# is resolved (`_plan_artifact`) and where every probe of it is taken
# (`_probe_plan_branch`) -- the three entry points that reading is judged behind -- one for a commit a
# round of this stage just made or left unreported, one for a publication in
# flight that a tick has to settle either way, and the pre-gate that decides
# which of those a crashed tick gets -- the remote read that settle spends its
# marker against (`_publication_on_the_branch`, since a local ref that never
# moved cannot say whether the commit went out), the commit-pinned PR lookup
# beside it (`_plan_pr_by_commit`, which searches every state and keeps the
# ones its CALLER names, since a branch name outlives every pull request opened
# on it and only the commit narrows the answer back to this publication's) and
# the open-PR reading next to it (`_plan_pr_overtaken`, for a
# head a human moved past this commit while still carrying it, where the push
# the lease refuses is a push there was never anything left to make) behind the
# one answer they compose into (`_settled_plan_pr`: every verdict the humans
# can leave on a pull request, since a merge, a close, and a head pushed past
# the commit all mean this publication has nothing left to push. WHEN a caller
# may act on each is its own -- the settle holds a close back until it has
# ruled out an operator's reset, because answering the stale park can mean
# closing the stray pull request as well as resetting the branch). The two
# entry points ask about different commits -- the tip on
# disk, and the tip the marker names when a rebuilt checkout is on somebody
# else's head. Then the fetch
# those two readings and the lease below them share (`_readable_remote_tip`,
# since each asks a local ancestry question about an id that came off the
# remote, and an id this store cannot resolve answers the same "no" a real
# divergence does), with the recording both of them end on
# (`_record_landed_plan`, since pushing a branch a merge deleted, asking for a
# second pull request on a commit already in the base, and sending an older SHA
# over somebody's own work are the three things that would otherwise follow)
# and the hold neither of them can be answered without
# (`_hold_unreadable_plan_pr`, for a commit list GitHub declined to serve,
# where the marker is written and nothing else so a round's session id is not
# lost to a read that has to be taken again), the settle for a marker whose tip
# has moved (`_settle_moved_marker`, which is every reading that acts ON the
# record rather than on the commit it names),
# the refusal that settle
# writes once, the
# publication they share (`_publish_plan`), the found-or-opened PR with its
# title, its body, and the attribution both that body and the reuse of a PR
# opened elsewhere are checked against -- plus the one question every path
# that touches a pull request asks first (`_attributable_plan`: there has to
# be a conversation to name, and the adoption reaches a PR ahead of the push's
# own refusal) -- and the records the whole thing leaves.
# Splitting the entry points from the publication they share would put what may
# be published and what publishing does in two modules, and the two would then
# have to agree on the order that makes a half-finished publication
# recoverable. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/discussion/terminal.py is the one place a
# discussion is decided to be over, and it carries 3 members past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7: the entry point that orders the readings, the two ways a pull
# request is found (`_plan_pr` off the records, `_interrupted_plan_pr` off the
# marker a crash left instead) with the ref resolution both share
# (`_plan_branch`), the arc the humans' verdict names (`_finalize_by_pr_state`)
# and the drain that applies it to a recorded pull request (`_drain_plan_pr`),
# the closed-issue reading that spends the marker (`_finalize_closed_discussion`)
# with the record it has to make first (`_record_recovered_pr`), the ending a
# close with no pull request behind it earns (`_reject_closed_discussion`), and
# the issue-state read they gate on (`_issue_closed`). Splitting the two lookups
# from the arcs they feed would put "which pull request is this issue's" in a
# second module from "what its verdict means", and those two disagreeing is
# exactly how a human's rejection gets republished over. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/discussion/run.py is one owner for a round and
# the record it opens on, and carries 9 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7:
# the two probes read before anything is prepared (`_stranded_worktree_state`,
# `_round_anchor_moved`) with the branch-tip fallback one of them needs
# (`_recorded_branch_moved`), the checkout the round runs in
# (`_ensure_round_worktree`) and the question of where to restore it from when
# it is gone (`_publication_on_the_remote`), the pre-spawn write that settles
# it and records
# what it opened on (`_open_round_checkout`) together with the authenticated
# base read that write pins (`_pinned_base_sha`), the round itself
# (`_open_discussion_round`), and the identity it runs under
# (`_record_round_session`). Every one of them exists for the same write: what a later
# tick may attribute to this round is decided by what was recorded before it
# spawned, so splitting the probes from the record they are compared against
# would put one attribution across two modules. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/implementing/read_only_relabel.py is one owner
# for the guard both read-only stages' parks are screened by AND for keeping
# what that guard handed over in step with the plan PR until a developer
# publishes, so it carries 16 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 13
# imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. The six that keep it in step belong
# with the guard rather than beside it: the reconcile
# (`_reconcile_open_plan_handoff`) reads the state the handoff left
# (`_accepted_handoff_baseline`), asks whether a developer has published on it
# since (`_handoff_unspent`), and takes the same reading of the same pull
# request (`_read_plan_pr`, split out because the guard knows there is one from
# the path record and the reconcile from the record that retired it) to put the
# branch back on what it carries (`_readvance_plan_handoff`, through the
# guard's own `_inherited_tip`, behind a marker `_spend_handoff_anchor` retires
# when the move it describes has nothing left to make). Split off, the rule
# that decides where a developer starts would live in two modules for one
# handoff -- and the marker that says the branch is this stage's rather than a
# developer's would be written in one and read in the other. The extra
# members and the extra import are the same thing: the
# two stages ask different questions of the same branch -- ahead-of-base for a
# question park, which never finishes on a branch carrying anything, and
# unchanged-since-the-round-anchor for a discussion park, which is allowed to
# open on the commits an issue arrived with -- so the owner is typed by the
# discussion stage's key vocabulary as well as implementing's own, and pays for
# it several times over: `_uncertified_commits` asks the branch question,
# `_tip_is_uncertified` decides which of the two readings answers it (a merged
# plan takes the older one back, since its own handoff moves the branch to the
# base before the write that records where it landed), and
# `_relabel_remediation` answers the one that follows from it, since an issue
# whose branch legitimately carries a PR's commits cannot be told to reset to
# base or delete the branch. Splitting the screen from the refusal it feeds
# would put one relabel's decision across two modules. WPS201 and WPS202 are
# waived.
#
# Three implementing stage owners and one question stage owner sit past the
# same ceiling -- `implementing/continue_command.py`, `implementing/spawn.py`,
# and `question/outcomes.py` at 13, and `implementing/publication.py` at 14,
# where the extra name is the `discussion` stage's plan-PR key: the record it
# retires is what kept implementing's merged-PR terminal from reading a plan
# being agreed to as work having landed, and this is the site that replaces
# that PR with one carrying the work. Each names the git owner behind every
# helper it runs,
# and those helpers are split across owners on purpose: the branch and worktree
# names, the checkout creation and its commit probe, the HEAD and dirty probes,
# the authenticated push, the commit-subject probe, the PR title builders, and
# the auto-rebase park vocabulary are seven owners between them, so a module
# that resolves a branch, recreates a checkout, and reads HEAD pays three
# imports for one paragraph of code. Reaching them through a single re-export
# site instead would put a module that defines nothing between the caller and
# the helper it runs, so WPS201 is waived for those files.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/implementing/publication.py carries 9 members
# past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 for one step that has to happen in one order:
# the agent message trimmed to fit a body (`_format_pr_agent_message`), the
# title taken from the branch's own first commit (`_derive_pr_title`), the
# session line the body states and the reuse checks a PR for
# (`_dev_pr_attribution`), the body itself (`_build_pr_body`), the
# found-or-opened PR (`_reuse_or_open_pr`) with the rewrite that makes a PR
# opened elsewhere -- an inherited plan PR most of all -- describe the work
# just pushed onto it (`_attribute_reused_pr`), the handoff write
# (`_advance_to_validating`) and the counters it spends
# (`_reset_implementing_counters`), and the entry point that sequences them
# (`_on_commits`). Splitting the reuse from the body it rewrites would put one
# PR's description across two modules, and splitting the handoff from the push
# would break the order that makes a crashed tick recoverable. WPS202 is
# waived.
#
# Three fixing stage owners name more than the same WPS201 ceiling of 12.
# `resume.py` names 22: it spans one dev run end to end, so it is typed by the
# engine's comment, drift, message, prompt, and usage surfaces, the four
# sibling owners it reads bookmarks, watermarks, records, and keys from, the
# dev resume it borrows from the implementing owners and the dev-fix
# disposition and stranded-fix probe from the validating ones, the comment
# timestamp the debounce borrows from in_review, and the git owners that name,
# restore, and measure the worktree it resumes in. `handler.py` names 15 because
# the order one tick asks its questions in is the contract, so it is typed by
# every owner an answer can come from plus the engine's guard and terminal
# surfaces its preflight runs first. `parked.py` names 13 because a parked tick
# is decided against every claimant of the reply at once: base-sync's park
# reasons, the engine's comment and message surfaces, the four sibling owners
# it reads bookmarks, the continue command, the drift reroute, and the keys
# from, the validating recovery and its park vocabulary, and the label a
# recovered park lands back on. Splitting any of them by import would put one
# tick's decisions across two modules. WPS201 is waived for those files.
#
# Four conflicts stage owners name more than the same WPS201 ceiling of 12.
# `resume.py` names 18 for the same reason as fixing's: it is the single entry
# point all three dev resumes on a mid-rebase branch go through, so it is typed
# by the engine's comment, drift, guard, message, prompt, and usage surfaces,
# the five sibling owners it reads the worktree probe, the records, the counter
# keys, the disposition, and the transitions from, the dev resume it borrows
# from the implementing owners and the body-edit disposition from the validating
# ones, base-sync's park reasons, the trust filter, and the HEAD owner it
# anchors each resume against. The other three pay the same span in git owners:
# `publication.py` names 14 and `outcomes.py` 13 because a rebase disposition
# names one owner per probe it reads and one per branch it publishes -- HEAD and
# dirty files, the branch name, the leased push, and the mid-rebase check --
# alongside the engine, sibling, and implementing surfaces the park and the
# handoff go through, and `routing.py` names 13 because the reconciliation it
# routes reads the branch name and the ahead/behind measurement before handing
# the worktree to any of its seven siblings. Splitting any of them by import
# would put one round's decisions across two modules. WPS201 is waived for
# those files.
#
# orchestrator/observability/analytics/config.py is one owner for everything
# the analytics and trajectory sinks are configured by -- the parse of the six
# environment knobs, the settings view every adapter reads one back through
# (on its own captured holder or on whichever the holder's name resolves to),
# and the read-path URL fallback -- so it carries 11 members past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7. The disable sentinels are one vocabulary across three of those
# knobs, and the values an adapter reads back are the values these parsers
# produced, so splitting the parse away from either half would let what "off"
# spells and what it costs drift apart. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/observability/analytics/query/rollup_reads.py is the read family
# hub with the widest result span, so it names 16 imports past the WPS201
# ceiling of 12. The list is the union of what one family is built from and
# hands back: one projection owner per public read, the keyword vocabulary those
# reads are bound through, and one import per result family under
# orchestrator/observability/analytics/query/ -- a hub that returns an activity
# cell, a cost breakdown, and a run row names the owner of each. Reaching them
# through a single re-export site instead would put a module that defines
# nothing between a reader and the class it constructs, so WPS201 is waived for
# that file.
#
# orchestrator/observability/usage/trajectory_codex_items.py is one owner for
# what a codex stream item normalizes to -- the record the frames of one item
# fold into, one normalizer per item type the exec stream reports, and the
# dispatch over them -- so it carries 8 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7.
# Every normalizer answers the same three questions about that one record:
# which of an item's own fields is the invocation, which is the outcome, and
# which frame is allowed to contribute either -- the completing one for a
# command or a plan, the terminal `status` for a patch, whichever filled the
# field last for a search. Splitting them by item type would leave the record
# they share and the dispatch that fills it in a module none of the answers is
# readable from, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/agents/sessions.py is one owner for both JSONL parsing surfaces
# -- session-id extraction and Claude final-message extraction -- and each
# surface decomposes into small single-purpose helpers, so the module carries
# 11 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7. orchestrator/agents/processes.py is
# one owner for the shared process registry and the subprocess-group lifecycle
# -- registration, bounded draining, timeout classification, and SIGTERM/SIGKILL
# escalation -- so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling.
# orchestrator/git/authentication.py owns everything the PAT travels through --
# per-repository token resolution, the askpass session and its detached
# environment, the two authenticated fetches, the authenticated read of a
# remote branch's tip, and the lease-pinned push -- so it
# carries 11 members past the same ceiling. Splitting any of them out would put
# a raw token on a module boundary. All three single-owner groupings are
# deliberate, so WPS202 is waived for those files.
#
# orchestrator/git/worktrees/creation.py owns every way a per-issue checkout
# comes into existence or is put where a PR's reviewed state now is -- the two
# creators, the unpushed-work probe they both gate on, the reported fetch they
# restore through, the start point a restore picks, and the handoff anchor with
# the target choice, the base fallback, and the ref move under it -- so it
# carries 10 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7. Each is one decision about
# which ref may be trusted,
# and two of them share the rule that only decides right together: the base is
# reserved for a branch the REMOTE says is gone, never for one a fetch merely
# failed to bring. Splitting them would put that rule in two places to drift
# apart, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/git/verification/probes.py owns every read over what an agent
# run left in a worktree -- the HEAD snapshot and the question of whether HEAD
# is the branch a caller is about to publish to, the status form and the path
# list
# built from it, the porcelain parse and the suppressed-index read that form
# answers with, the tree entry at a permitted path, and the two commit-level
# questions a caller has to answer before it records an object id or overwrites
# a ref with one -- so it carries 11 members past the same ceiling. Each is one
# hardened `git` invocation whose failure mode is decided in the same place its
# flags are, and the callers pick between them by which of those failures is
# safe for them, so splitting the family would separate a probe from the answer
# beside it that it exists to be told apart from. The two the status form is
# built out of are the same rule again at one level down: what a clean tree
# means is decided by the parse and the index read together, and a caller can
# reach neither on its own. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/github/pull_requests.py owns the whole pull-request surface --
# branch/base lookup by open state and the commit-pinned one beside it that
# widens to any state and matches on the commits a pull request CARRIES rather
# than the head it is on (what a publication whose PR was amended or merged
# before anything recorded its number is found by, with the walk it is built
# from split off so the enumeration's own failure -- a page that never came
# back is not a branch with no pull requests on it -- has one place to be
# caught), creation, comments,
# body rewrite,
# labeling, retrieval,
# the SHA-pinned merge, and the head-branch delete -- so its client mixin
# carries 11 methods past the WPS214 ceiling of 7. Splitting the merge-side
# mutations out would hand the composed client two mixins for one owner, so
# WPS214 is waived here.
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_coordinator.py drives one
# late adjudication in order -- the live-generation gate, the frozen-evidence
# proof, the plan-PR hold, the completed-result short circuit, the
# retry-budgeted spawn, the accounting a pre-spawn write holds back, the
# shared post-run pause / timeout / interruption contract, and the read-only
# proof over the candidate worktree, the gates that stop a tick before any
# agent could run, and the guard every completion passes before anything acts
# on what it left -- so it carries 14 members past the
# WPS202 ceiling of 7 and reaches 26 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. The members are the steps of one
# ordered contract in which each persists what it reached before it acts, and
# the imports are what that order costs: the late domain's state and
# vocabularies, the engine's guards and usage accounting, the git worktree the
# frozen candidate lives in and the probes that prove it unmoved, and the late
# owners beside it. What one finished reply BECOMES is already split off onto
# `late_outcome.py`, and what a guarded verdict EARNS onto
# `late_settlement.py`; splitting the order itself would put the
# persist-before-side-effect sequence in two places to drift apart, so WPS201
# and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_outcome.py is the other half
# of that pair -- what a finished reply becomes, the announcement a recorded
# question is reconciled by, the parks every late exit hands the issue
# back through -- staged for the owner read to release, said at once where
# nothing has been paid for yet, released anyway where no later attempt
# would ever say them, or re-said at the top of a later tick when the comment
# that should have said them was refused -- including the retirement of one an
# attempt has answered, the one write that closes a completion by making what
# it left durable together with the owner read it now owes, the reconciliation
# that discharges an obligation the thread shows was already met, and the three
# emissions that report a verdict, a typed failure, and a cancellation -- so
# it carries 23 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 15 imports past
# the WPS201 ceiling of 12. They share
# one ordering rule, that the durable write goes out before the external
# effect, and the parks and emissions are where that rule is kept; separating a
# park from the record it commits is exactly the drift the rule exists to
# prevent. Which park is standing, whether its sentence was ever said, and
# whether anything will supersede it are three readings of that same rule, so
# they are asked here rather than beside the field they read. The
# imports are the union of what the rule touches: the late domain's state,
# events, telemetry, and the phase a completion's own write stamps, the reply
# parser, the run record, the undelivered notice a park owes, and the engine
# guard a park goes through. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/late_split/state.py is the whole round trip a late
# generation makes through the pinned comment, and it carries 8 members past
# the WPS202 ceiling of 7: the read, the write, the clear that defines what
# "late mode off" means, and the five composers behind the write -- the
# evidence half, the two ledgers, the rule that a verbatim copy outranks a
# typed view, and the JSON one obligation is spelled as. They are one contract:
# what a field means when it is unset is decided by the same owner that decides
# what it reads back as, and splitting them would let the two disagree about a
# key a live issue already carries. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/late_split/models.py is the frozen record one late
# generation IS, and it carries 8 methods past the WPS214 ceiling of 7. Four
# are the questions every reader asks of it -- whether a cycle was ever
# recorded, whether the measurement trips the ceiling, whether the lineage has
# room, and whether an obligation on it is one this binary cannot type -- and
# four are the transforms that return a NEW record rather than mutating this
# one: an obligation recorded, a consumer recorded, the ordered child register
# replaced, and the cancellation stamped. Splitting them would put the record
# and the only legal ways to change it in two modules, which is exactly the
# separation a frozen record exists to prevent. WPS214 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_snapshot.py owns the whole
# snapshot step -- the ref this generation's identity names, the durable
# obligation written ahead of the push and again behind the proof, the
# create-or-verify and the fetch that proves it, and the one shape every
# refusal takes -- and names 13 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. They are
# what "snapshot-first" costs: the namespace and the transport that write the
# ref, the domain's typed failure, resource vocabulary, and refusal, the event
# and the dual emission that report one external resource, and the outcome
# owner every park and persist rides. WPS201 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_children.py owns the whole
# child half of a split -- the walk that adopts what the parent already
# records, the umbrella flag and count written before the first child, the one
# write that records a child as a child, a consumer, and an obligation at
# once, the seed that adds an ancestry without replacing a child's own work,
# and the body and the lineage that child is born with -- so it carries 13
# members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 15 imports past the WPS201
# ceiling of 12. The order is the crash-safety contract, and the imports are
# what a child needs to exist: the lineage record it inherits, the depth
# bound, the phase, the two ledger vocabularies, the initial split's own child
# labels, the stage's pinned keys and split plan, and the outcome owner every
# park and persist rides. Splitting it would put "created" and "recorded" in
# two modules, so WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_cleanup.py owns everything a
# split still owes a remote once its children are running, so it carries 14
# members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 15 imports past the WPS201
# ceiling of 12. The members are one question asked twice over: which branches
# and which refs are unsettled, whether the consumers a ref is kept for are
# terminal (and what "terminal" is for one of them), the delete for each kind
# and the one write that records either answer, the obligation the transaction
# records ahead of its own first attempt, the pass that settles what can be
# settled and what it asked, the subset that may not be left behind, the two
# emissions, and the decision the umbrella's terminal reads. Splitting them
# would separate "may this be deleted" from "delete it", which is the pair the
# whole reclamation rule is. The imports are what asking costs: the pinned
# generation the ledger is read off, the resource vocabulary it is typed by,
# the snapshot transport and the client the two deletes go through, the events
# and telemetry one reclamation is reported on, and the child scan the terminal
# rule is proved against. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_transaction.py is the order
# a cleared split runs in -- the two refusals no step below could repair, the
# snapshot, the children, the forward links and the stamp that stops them
# repeating, the supersession of the held plan PR, the retirement that hands
# the parent to `umbrella`, the activation behind it, and the branch
# obligation reconciled after -- so it carries 13 members past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7 and names 20 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. Every step
# is what makes the next one safe, and the imports are the union of what the
# order touches: the snapshot and children owners, the hold's release, the
# worktree teardown and branch resolution, the comment poster and the stamp
# clock, the domain's failure, phase, resource, and generation records with
# the events and telemetry that report them, and the label the parent is
# handed on under. WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_settlement.py is what a
# guarded verdict earns -- the announcement a question owes the issue, the
# handoff a split makes to the transaction that creates its children, and the
# whole of a `single`: the plan-PR hold released, the pull request reconciled
# against the measured commit (with the settled pointer that would otherwise
# end the issue dropped, and the refusal both of those fail closed to), the
# exemption written, and the generation retired behind the hand-back -- so it
# carries 8 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and names 13 imports past the
# WPS201 ceiling of 12. They are one ordered reconciliation in which each step
# is what makes the next one safe, and the imports are what that order costs:
# the branch the exact-commit lookup is taken on, the lookup's own unreadable
# sentinel, the domain's exemption and defensive field readers, the hold owner
# and the outcome owner beside it, and the label the candidate is handed back
# under. Splitting it would put the persist-before-effect sequence in two
# modules, so WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_owner.py owns the whole
# post-agent owner read -- the question of whether the claim the read may not
# be taken without is already standing, the reconciliation that takes an owed
# one again, the guarded read itself, the
# fetch and the state behind it, the three answers it can give (retire the
# check and say so, cancel the cycle, leave the read owed), and the one
# follow-up a park that healed owes the thread -- so it carries 10 members
# past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7. They are one contract read end to end: what the pending marker
# means is decided by what writes it, what a follow-up is owed for is decided
# by which park was taken, and splitting the read away from either would put
# the fail-closed rule in two modules. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_hold.py owns the whole
# cycle-marked hold -- the marker and the body it is written as, the spelling
# an earlier binary wrote and the one question that recognizes either as ours,
# the discussion provenance that decides whether a recorded pull request may be
# touched at all, the fetch that fails closed, the take / re-apply /
# refuse reconciliation over them, and the release that reads the same
# reconciliation backwards, with the reusability of the pull request deciding
# what a refused release may stop -- so it carries 13 members past the WPS202
# ceiling of 7 and names 13 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. Each is one
# question the one before it makes safe to ask, and the persist-before-mutate
# order runs through all of them; the ninth is the single read every one of
# those questions is answered from, since a lazy pull request fails on its
# first attribute rather than on its fetch. The last import is the run
# record's own budget, since what a hold may preserve is decided by what the
# record that starts the run still has to fit beside it. WPS201 and WPS202
# are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_session.py owns the late
# run's whole pinned record -- the identity half and the completed-outcome
# half, each read back through the rules it was written under -- and the
# tracked spawn over it -- plus the budget that decides whether the comment
# could still hold the run's record beside a preserved pull-request body -- so
# it carries 12 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7 and reaches 17 imports
# past the WPS201 ceiling of 12: the late domain's defensive field readers and
# the two vocabularies a recorded result is typed by, the split validator a
# recorded manifest is held to, the pinned-state owner that renders the body
# being measured, the configured agent spec the first run falls back to, the
# tracked-run and trusted-thread owners the spawn goes through, and the prompt
# built beside it. Reading a key
# somewhere other than where it is written, or holding a recorded manifest to
# rules other than the ones the reply was held to, is what puts one contract
# in two places, so WPS201 and WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_models.py carries every
# record the late owners hand each other -- the tick's own subject, the hold,
# the run, one reply's verdict, what a fresh owner read said, the park a
# completion recorded but has not said yet, the split that reading cleared,
# what the requirements behind the frozen
# candidate hash to, what the humans have said since, and what the whole call
# did with its tick -- so it reaches 13 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7.
# They sit together because each crosses a boundary the call stack alone would
# lose it across, and splitting them by which owner happens to build one would
# make the shared vocabulary two vocabularies free to drift. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_guidance.py is one ordered
# decision table -- take a baseline, park requirements that moved, certify a
# frozen candidate, resume a stalled revision, reopen a categorized question,
# refuse a bare continue that answers none, and fold consumed conversation
# into the baseline -- so it carries 9 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7.
# Each branch is one row of the same table and every row shares the rule that
# drift outranks an answer; separating them would put that precedence in two
# places to disagree. WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/stages/decomposition/late_revision.py runs the whole
# developer revision a human's guidance buys -- consume the guidance, resume
# the locked session under the decomposing stage, prove the tree it left
# clean, freeze what it ends on, measure that commit again under the
# current ceiling, and read the owner once more past whatever the
# reconciliation made durable -- so it names 22 imports past the WPS201
# ceiling of 12.
# The imports are what that arc costs and nothing else: the shared developer
# resume and the prompt notes it is built from, the engine's comment, guard,
# and usage owners, the worktree path and the two probes that prove the
# checkout, the size measurement, the late domain's phase and typed failure
# with the telemetry they are reported on, and the late owners beside it.
# Splitting the arc would put the consume-before-spawn and prove-before-freeze
# orders in two modules, so WPS201 is waived. It carries 10 members past the
# WPS202 ceiling of 7 for the same reason: the arc's own steps, one of which
# is the owner read every end of it passes, plus the two
# that decide what an UNCHANGED commit means -- whether the run vouched for it
# with the marker the prompt asks for, and what the run said instead when it
# did not, which the park quotes to the human. Reading a commit nobody changed
# as an acknowledgment is the failure those two exist to prevent, and they
# belong beside the freeze that would otherwise accept it.
#
per-file-ignores =
    orchestrator/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/config/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/agents/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/github/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/scheduler/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
